<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Vienna Award</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> Award was an arbitration by Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a>, announced in Vienna on 2 November 1938, that transferred southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Carpathian Ruthenia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. It followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzhhorod">Uzhhorod</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukachevo">Mukachevo</a> went to Hungary, forcing the autonomous Carpatho-Ukrainian government to move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khust">Khust</a>; the award was annulled after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-vienna-award.jpg" alt="First Vienna Award" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jxxy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Territorial_gains_of_Hungary_1938-41_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>From 1933 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> coordinated its foreign policy closely with that of Nazi Germany, hoping to revise the borders set by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> of 1920. In June 1933 the Hungarian prime minister Gyula Gömbös met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>, and they concluded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> was a principal obstacle to a rearrangement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> of September 1938 partitioned Czechoslovakia and set a three-month period to settle Hungarian demands, but Hungary pressed for talks at once, with border conflicts and sabotage. On 5 October 1938 Hungarian troops attacked Czechoslovak positions near Jesenské, and paramilitaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ragged-guard">Rongyos Gárda</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Carpathian Ruthenia</a> on 6 October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> declared autonomy on 6 October. Czechoslovak and Hungarian delegations met at Komárno from 9 to 13 October 1938, the Czechoslovak side led by Jozef Tiso and the Hungarian by Kálmán Kánya and Pál Teleki. The talks deadlocked on 13 October, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> partly mobilised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> proposed the Ribbentrop line on 22 October, offering territory with 494,646 Hungarians and 168,632 Slovaks. Hungary rejected it and demanded arbitration, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had lost interest in Czechoslovakia.</p><p>The award was made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>'s Belvedere Palace on 2 November 1938 by the German foreign minister Joachim von Ribbentrop and the Italian foreign minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-ciano">Galeazzo Ciano</a>. Czechoslovakia gave up southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Carpathian Ruthenia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosice">Košice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzhhorod">Uzhhorod</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukachevo">Mukachevo</a>. Slovakia lost 10,390 square kilometres with 854,277 inhabitants by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1930, 503,980 of them Hungarians. Czechoslovakia also lost its direct railway link to Carpathian Ruthenia and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Tiso announced the result on the radio that evening and blamed the central government. The award led to anti-Hungarian feeling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, which Slovaks marked on each anniversary. In mid-March 1939 Hitler allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to occupy the rest of Carpathian Rus', which gave Hungary a common border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Only about 60,000 Hungarians remained in Slovakia, and the new border did not follow ethnic lines: only 8 of 79 villages around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosice">Košice</a> had a Hungarian majority. The Treaty of Paris, signed on 10 February 1947, declared the award null and void and restored the frontier of 1 January 1938, except for three villages south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Vienna Award” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through the <b>First Vienna Award</b> it regained southern Czechoslovakia, then occupied the Carpatho-Ukraine, and through the Second Vienna Award it gained Northern Transylvania, before joining the Axis powers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>First Vienna Award</b> gave Uzhhorod and the southern lowlands to the Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946), the government moved to Khust.</blockquote><a href="/state/carpatho-ukraine">Carpatho-Ukraine</a></li></ul></section>
